{"conjecture": "Software systems accumulate a load-bearing layer of implicit, cross-component invariants that are never written down because no single change introduces them; each locally-correct modification subtly broadens the set of assumptions future developers must hold, so the effective dimensionality of the system's hidden contract space grows super-linearly. This turns the codebase into a high-dimensional constraint satisfaction problem where the cost of verifying a change safely scales with the total number of surviving implicit invariants, not the size of the change itself."}
